1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,024
The shelling had not stopped
for nine days.

2
00:00:03,593 --> 00:00:09,547
Fifty feet beneath what had once been the capital of
a continent, the concrete sweated

3
00:00:09,567 --> 00:00:15,452
and shook, and the dust came down from the ceiling
in slow grey threads, like sand in

4
00:00:15,472 --> 00:00:19,411
an hourglass that someone had turned
over one final time.

5
00:00:19,981 --> 00:00:26,032
At the end of a corridor, behind a steel door, a
man sat on a small sofa and listened

6
00:00:26,052 --> 00:00:27,890
to the city die above him.

7
00:00:28,460 --> 00:00:34,170
He had started the war that killed tens of millions.
He had built the camps, and filled

8
00:00:34,190 --> 00:00:39,900
them. He had told a nation it was chosen, and then
he had marched it into fire. And now

9
00:00:39,920 --> 00:00:42,205
the fire had come home to find him.

10
00:00:42,776 --> 00:00:48,731
Nobody who was in that room ever described his last
thought. We only know how it ended.

11
00:00:52,601 --> 00:00:57,795
A single shot, muffled by concrete. And then, for
the man who fired it, nothing.

12
00:00:59,866 --> 00:01:05,338
History wrote its verdict in the ashes he left
behind. That should have been the end of

13
00:01:05,358 --> 00:01:05,969
the story.

14
00:01:07,739 --> 00:01:13,867
Somewhere else — in a world that does not exist, a
world with no Berlin and no bunker

15
00:01:13,887 --> 00:01:15,676
— a second man was dying.

16
00:01:16,246 --> 00:01:22,323
He was standing waist-deep in molten rock, in the
throat of a volcano, and he was still,

17
00:01:22,343 --> 00:01:23,570
impossibly, alive.

18
00:01:24,141 --> 00:01:30,306
He had been a scientist and a soldier, a traitor and
a god in his own estimation. He had

19
00:01:30,326 --> 00:01:36,633
been engineered to be superior, and he had believed it
with his whole heart. He had wanted

20
00:01:36,653 --> 00:01:42,468
to remake the human race in a single breath of a
virus. His name was Albert Wesker.

21
00:01:43,037 --> 00:01:48,937
And above him, on the rim of the rock, two people he
had underestimated for the last time

22
00:01:48,957 --> 00:01:51,199
lifted a pair of rocket launchers.

23
00:01:54,567 --> 00:01:56,838
Fire. Light. Then nothing again.

24
00:01:58,908 --> 00:02:03,615
Two deaths. Two men who never met, in two worlds
that should never have touched.

25
00:02:04,186 --> 00:02:06,902
This is the story of what
happened when they did.

26
00:02:25,673 --> 00:02:28,149
The building had no name worth
remembering.

27
00:02:28,718 --> 00:02:34,649
Four storeys of tired brick on the wrong side of an
industrial park, on the south bank

28
00:02:34,669 --> 00:02:40,600
of the Christina River. A parking lot with more
weeds than cars. A security booth with

29
00:02:40,620 --> 00:02:46,481
a space heater and a paperback. And above the glass
doors, in faded blue letters that

30
00:02:46,501 --> 00:02:52,640
had not been cleaned since the Obama administration,
a sign that read: Umbrella Research,

31
00:02:52,660 --> 00:02:53,678
Division Seven.

32
00:02:54,247 --> 00:02:59,851
Nobody in the city had ever wondered what happened
inside. There was no reason to. The

33
00:02:59,871 --> 00:03:05,474
company that owned it was dying, and the people who
worked in it knew it, and the rain

34
00:03:05,494 --> 00:03:11,425
came down on the flat roof the way it had come down on
a thousand other forgotten buildings

35
00:03:11,445 --> 00:03:13,778
in a thousand other forgotten towns.

36
00:03:14,348 --> 00:03:20,220
On the third floor, in a corner office with a water
stain shaped like Florida on the ceiling

37
00:03:20,240 --> 00:03:22,909
tile, a man was asleep in a leather chair.

38
00:03:23,479 --> 00:03:26,779
And then, between one second and the
next, he was not.

39
00:03:27,349 --> 00:03:33,332
He did not wake the way people wake. There was no
drift, no warmth, no slow return. He

40
00:03:33,352 --> 00:03:38,705
arrived — the way a gunshot arrives — whole,
and violent, and already moving.

41
00:03:39,276 --> 00:03:45,415
His elbow struck a ceramic mug on the desk. It
tipped, and fell, and he caught it an inch

42
00:03:45,435 --> 00:03:51,643
above the carpet, cold coffee still trembling inside
it, before he had consciously decided

43
00:03:51,663 --> 00:03:52,682
to move at all.

44
00:03:54,252 --> 00:03:56,929
He looked at his own hand,
holding the mug.

45
00:03:57,498 --> 00:04:03,919
It was a large hand. Pale. Steady. It was not the
hand he remembered. And it was, at the

46
00:04:03,939 --> 00:04:06,993
same time, exactly the hand he remembered.

47
00:04:08,364 --> 00:04:13,782
Two memories reached for the same body, like two men
grabbing for the same pistol in the

48
00:04:13,802 --> 00:04:14,091
dark.

49
00:04:14,662 --> 00:04:20,555
One remembered a bunker, a sofa, the smell of
cordite and concrete, and a nation that

50
00:04:20,575 --> 00:04:22,641
had followed him over a cliff.

51
00:04:23,211 --> 00:04:29,293
The other remembered a laboratory in the Arklay
Mountains, a mansion full of monsters,

52
00:04:29,313 --> 00:04:32,982
a virus that had rewritten his
blood, and a volcano.

53
00:04:33,552 --> 00:04:36,571
Neither memory was a dream. Both were his.

54
00:04:38,341 --> 00:04:44,265
He set the mug down, very precisely, in the exact
ring of dried coffee it had left on

55
00:04:44,285 --> 00:04:50,280
the desk. Then he stood, and the chair rolled back,
and he walked out of the office to

56
00:04:50,300 --> 00:04:51,260
find a mirror.

57
00:04:51,829 --> 00:04:56,358
The washroom light buzzed and flickered twice
before it decided to work.

58
00:04:56,928 --> 00:05:02,929
The man in the mirror was tall, and pale, and blond,
with his hair swept back from a hard,

59
00:05:02,949 --> 00:05:08,615
handsome face. Mid-thirties, perhaps, though the
eyes were older than that. He wore a

60
00:05:08,635 --> 00:05:14,769
black shirt, dark trousers, and the kind of calm that
belongs to a predator that has already

61
00:05:14,789 --> 00:05:15,839
eaten this week.

62
00:05:16,411 --> 00:05:22,580
He leaned closer. And the pupils, as he watched,
narrowed into vertical slits, and flared

63
00:05:22,600 --> 00:05:23,972
a dull, burning red.

64
00:05:24,661 --> 00:05:26,721
This is not my face.

65
00:05:27,242 --> 00:05:28,279
No. It's mine.

66
00:05:28,798 --> 00:05:34,291
The voices did not come from his mouth. They came
from somewhere behind the eyes, and

67
00:05:34,311 --> 00:05:39,805
they spoke at the same time, and for a long moment
the man in the mirror said nothing

68
00:05:39,825 --> 00:05:43,307
at all while two histories argued
over the same skull.

69
00:05:43,997 --> 00:05:47,593
Where is this? What is this place?
What have you done?

70
00:05:48,113 --> 00:05:53,776
I haven't done anything. I died. So, I suspect, did
you. Congratulations. We appear to

71
00:05:53,796 --> 00:05:55,163
have been — combined.

72
00:05:55,684 --> 00:05:57,541
I do not accept that.

73
00:05:58,062 --> 00:05:59,892
The mirror doesn't care what you accept.

74
00:06:01,411 --> 00:06:07,025
He gripped the edge of the sink, experimentally, and
felt the porcelain creak under his

75
00:06:07,045 --> 00:06:09,485
fingers, and let go before it cracked.

76
00:06:10,055 --> 00:06:15,932
The body was not human. Not entirely. He could feel
the Prototype Virus in it, the way

77
00:06:15,952 --> 00:06:22,035
a pilot feels an engine through the seat — patient
and enormous and hungry, woven through

78
00:06:22,055 --> 00:06:27,933
every cell. Strength that could fold a car door.
Speed that would look, to an ordinary

79
00:06:27,953 --> 00:06:33,488
eye, like a man simply ceasing to be in one place
and beginning to be in another.

80
00:06:34,059 --> 00:06:39,471
The Wesker half of the mind knew all of this the way
you know your own name. The other

81
00:06:39,491 --> 00:06:45,410
half learned it in that moment, and felt something it
had not felt since nineteen thirty-nine.

82
00:06:46,579 --> 00:06:47,632
Appetite.

83
00:06:49,702 --> 00:06:51,420
That was when the light appeared.

84
00:06:51,990 --> 00:06:58,178
It was not a reflection. It hung in the air a foot
in front of the mirror: a thin pane

85
00:06:58,198 --> 00:07:04,457
of cold, white glass, edged in crimson, with letters
typing themselves across it one by

86
00:07:04,477 --> 00:07:04,746
one.

87
00:07:06,065 --> 00:07:09,748
Host confirmed. Integration complete.

88
00:07:12,305 --> 00:07:13,369
Secondary.

89
00:07:13,889 --> 00:07:16,191
Don't take it personally. It's a machine.

90
00:07:16,709 --> 00:07:19,326
A second panel slid out beneath the first.

91
00:07:20,648 --> 00:07:21,719
Status.

92
00:07:26,798 --> 00:07:31,653
He read it the way a general reads a map of
a battle he intends to win.

93
00:07:32,223 --> 00:07:38,247
He knew instinctively what the numbers meant. Ten
was an ordinary man. Twenty was the

94
00:07:38,267 --> 00:07:44,362
very edge of what a human body could do. Thirty and
above was something else entirely.

95
00:07:45,052 --> 00:07:47,528
Charisma twenty-four. That's
you, I assume.

96
00:07:48,048 --> 00:07:50,332
A nation followed that voice.

97
00:07:50,853 --> 00:07:52,902
Into a bunker. Yes. I remember.

98
00:07:54,052 --> 00:08:02,827
Warning. Viral stability, seventy-one percent. At
forty percent, cellular consumption

99
00:08:02,847 --> 00:08:03,552
begins.

100
00:08:05,141 --> 00:08:09,653
For the first time since he had woken, the man
in the mirror went very still.

101
00:08:10,223 --> 00:08:16,031
The Wesker half knew this problem. In his own world,
he had kept the virus leashed with

102
00:08:16,051 --> 00:08:21,926
an injected serum, measured and precise. There was no
serum here. There was no here, not

103
00:08:21,946 --> 00:08:26,482
yet. Just a building, and a clock, and a
number going the wrong way.

104
00:08:27,802 --> 00:08:36,088
The Shop is locked. Points are earned through
conquest, loyalty, superior action, and

105
00:08:36,108 --> 00:08:38,238
the defeat of enemies.

106
00:08:39,827 --> 00:08:42,197
Conquest. Loyalty. Superior action.

107
00:08:42,716 --> 00:08:44,916
It is describing a state.

108
00:08:45,436 --> 00:08:49,021
It's describing a company. Same thing,
if you do it properly.

109
00:08:50,171 --> 00:09:00,065
Mission issued. The Director's Chair. Secure absolute
control of Umbrella Research, Division

110
00:09:00,085 --> 00:09:02,005
Seven. Seven days.

111
00:09:03,595 --> 00:09:09,163
He read the mission twice. Then he smiled, very
slightly, for the first time in his new

112
00:09:09,183 --> 00:09:09,485
life.

113
00:09:10,054 --> 00:09:15,777
It was not a nice smile. It was the smile of a man
who has been handed a pistol in a room

114
00:09:15,797 --> 00:09:18,487
full of people who don't know it's loaded.

115
00:09:20,557 --> 00:09:22,772
There was a knock at the washroom door.

116
00:09:23,461 --> 00:09:25,656
Doctor Wesker? You in there?

117
00:09:26,174 --> 00:09:32,029
The voice belonged to a man in his sixties, gravel
over gravel, the voice of someone who

118
00:09:32,049 --> 00:09:36,502
had spent a great deal of his life telling
younger men to get down.

119
00:09:37,072 --> 00:09:43,079
Wesker opened the door. And the knowledge was simply
there, waiting for him, the way the

120
00:09:43,099 --> 00:09:48,969
stats had been: Walter Pruitt. Night security.
Sixty-four. Army, twenty-two years. Two

121
00:09:48,989 --> 00:09:54,996
tours he never talked about. A wife who had died in
the spring. He worked nights because

122
00:09:55,016 --> 00:09:56,571
he didn't sleep anyway.

123
00:09:57,261 --> 00:10:02,254
Heard something fall. Thought maybe you'd keeled over.
You been here since yesterday afternoon,

124
00:10:02,274 --> 00:10:02,992
you know that?

125
00:10:03,513 --> 00:10:04,665
I know that, Walter.

126
00:10:05,184 --> 00:10:10,983
He had not raised his voice. He had not done anything
at all. He simply stood in the doorway,

127
00:10:11,003 --> 00:10:16,364
a head taller than the guard, and looked at him —
and Walter Pruitt, who had once been

128
00:10:16,384 --> 00:10:21,932
shot at by professionals and had not flinched, took
half a step back without knowing why.

129
00:10:22,623 --> 00:10:25,850
Right. Well. You want coffee? Pot's
old, but it's hot.

130
00:10:26,370 --> 00:10:29,230
No. Thank you. Go home early
tonight. I'll lock up.

131
00:10:29,750 --> 00:10:32,286
Can't do that, Doc. Company rules.

132
00:10:32,806 --> 00:10:38,844
The company, Walter, is in administration in London,
and the people who wrote its rules

133
00:10:38,864 --> 00:10:44,832
are currently arguing over who gets the office
furniture. As of tonight, the rules are

134
00:10:44,852 --> 00:10:48,314
mine. Go home. Get some sleep. You
won't, but try.

135
00:10:49,833 --> 00:10:55,141
Walter looked at him for a long moment. There was
something in the old guard's face —

136
00:10:55,161 --> 00:11:00,469
not fear, exactly. Recognition. The expression of a
man who had served under officers

137
00:11:00,489 --> 00:11:05,860
his whole life and had learned to tell, instantly,
the difference between the ones who

138
00:11:05,880 --> 00:11:08,681
were playing at it and the ones
who were not.

139
00:11:09,370 --> 00:11:10,335
Yes, sir.

140
00:11:10,855 --> 00:11:13,626
He had not called anyone sir in
seven years.

141
00:11:14,196 --> 00:11:20,365
He went. And halfway down the corridor, alone with
the rain, he said quietly to nobody

142
00:11:20,385 --> 00:11:20,869
at all:

143
00:11:21,558 --> 00:11:22,904
Different animal.

144
00:11:24,926 --> 00:11:30,701
Back in the corner office, the man who was now Albert
Wesker sat down at a computer that

145
00:11:30,721 --> 00:11:33,007
was nine years old and ran like it.

146
00:11:33,576 --> 00:11:39,350
The Wesker half of the mind knew what to do with it.
The other half watched, fascinated,

147
00:11:39,370 --> 00:11:41,720
as the screen filled with the world.

148
00:11:42,289 --> 00:11:48,533
It took him less than an hour to understand where he
was. An Earth, but not Wesker's Earth.

149
00:11:48,553 --> 00:11:54,728
There was no Raccoon City here. No Umbrella
Corporation that had ever mattered. No mansion

150
00:11:54,748 --> 00:12:00,648
in the Arklay Mountains, no Redfields, no Spencer.
He searched his own name, and found

151
00:12:00,668 --> 00:12:05,673
a dentist in Ohio and a high-school wrestler
in Oregon, and nothing else.

152
00:12:06,361 --> 00:12:08,614
Nobody. Nobody has ever heard of me.

153
00:12:09,134 --> 00:12:12,413
Then the other half of the mind
typed a different name.

154
00:12:13,982 --> 00:12:15,725
He read for a long time.

155
00:12:16,296 --> 00:12:22,350
He read about the war he had started, and how it
ended. He read the numbers — the soldiers,

156
00:12:22,370 --> 00:12:28,025
the cities, the six million murdered in the camps,
and the millions more. He read the

157
00:12:28,045 --> 00:12:33,899
testimony, and the photographs, and the verdicts at
Nuremberg. He read that his name had

158
00:12:33,919 --> 00:12:37,972
become the single word the whole human
species used for evil.

159
00:12:38,542 --> 00:12:44,546
He read that he had lost. Not narrowly. Totally.
His country had been cut in half and

160
00:12:44,566 --> 00:12:51,138
rebuilt by the men he had despised, and it had become,
of all things, prosperous and peaceful

161
00:12:51,158 --> 00:12:51,776
and dull.

162
00:12:53,546 --> 00:12:56,504
If you were hoping he felt
remorse, he did not.

163
00:12:57,073 --> 00:13:03,565
What he felt, reading the verdict of history on a
cheap monitor in the rain, was not guilt.

164
00:13:03,585 --> 00:13:09,719
It was humiliation. The fury of a man who has been
told that he was not merely wicked,

165
00:13:09,739 --> 00:13:10,506
but beaten.

166
00:13:11,197 --> 00:13:12,904
They made me a word.

167
00:13:13,424 --> 00:13:17,536
They made you a warning. There's a
difference. A word can still be used.

168
00:13:18,057 --> 00:13:24,096
I will not be pitied by a laboratory rat who was
killed by two soldiers in a hole.

169
00:13:24,615 --> 00:13:30,211
Then let's be precise, shall we? We both lost. You
had a nation, and you lost it. I had

170
00:13:30,231 --> 00:13:35,697
a virus that could have remade the species, and I
lost that too. And do you know why?

171
00:13:35,717 --> 00:13:36,408
Both of us?

172
00:13:37,726 --> 00:13:43,681
The man at the desk leaned back in the chair, and the
monitor glow slid across his face,

173
00:13:43,701 --> 00:13:48,298
and for a moment both halves of him were
perfectly, terribly silent.

174
00:13:48,989 --> 00:13:50,863
You lost because they saw you coming.

175
00:13:52,263 --> 00:13:57,442
You told them. You wrote it in a book. You shouted
it into microphones. You put it on

176
00:13:57,462 --> 00:14:02,824
banners. You gave the whole world ten years' notice.
And the moment they understood what

177
00:14:02,844 --> 00:14:05,087
you were, they united to destroy you.

178
00:14:05,488 --> 00:14:11,217
And I did the same thing. I stood on a stage and
announced my plan to the only two people

179
00:14:11,237 --> 00:14:12,380
who could stop it.

180
00:14:12,899 --> 00:14:15,120
And what would you do instead?

181
00:14:15,639 --> 00:14:16,850
Nothing they could see.

182
00:14:18,870 --> 00:14:20,117
The rain went on.

183
00:14:20,687 --> 00:14:25,398
Somewhere in the building a freezer compressor
kicked on, and hummed, and settled.

184
00:14:25,967 --> 00:14:32,215
And inside one skull, two of the most dangerous minds
that had ever existed — in any world

185
00:14:32,235 --> 00:14:35,279
— came, for the first time, to
an agreement.

186
00:14:37,350 --> 00:14:41,225
Dawn came up over the Christina River,
grey and then gold.

187
00:14:41,796 --> 00:14:48,037
The rain stopped. The industrial park woke up in the
ordinary way: a delivery truck, a

188
00:14:48,057 --> 00:14:54,444
gull, a man in a hi-vis vest yawning by a loading
dock. Nobody looked at the third-floor

189
00:14:54,464 --> 00:14:59,759
window of the brick building with the faded
sign. There was no reason to.

190
00:15:00,327 --> 00:15:06,058
Behind that window, a man in a long black coat
stood with his hands folded behind his

191
00:15:06,078 --> 00:15:08,222
back and watched the day arrive.

192
00:15:08,793 --> 00:15:14,952
He had seven days. He had no money, no army, no
name that anyone recognised. He had a

193
00:15:14,972 --> 00:15:21,278
company that was barely a company, a virus that was
slowly eating him, and a machine in

194
00:15:21,298 --> 00:15:25,786
the air that promised him the world in
exchange for taking it.

195
00:15:26,355 --> 00:15:30,762
He picked up the sunglasses from the desk, and
looked at them, and put them on.

196
00:15:31,451 --> 00:15:33,387
They didn't see you coming
either, you know.

197
00:15:33,907 --> 00:15:34,814
Who?

198
00:15:35,333 --> 00:15:39,081
The world. In nineteen thirty-three. They
thought you were a clown.

199
00:15:39,600 --> 00:15:40,668
And then?

200
00:15:41,188 --> 00:15:44,937
Behind the dark lenses, the eyes
flared red, and faded.

201
00:15:45,626 --> 00:15:46,630
And then they learned.

202
00:15:48,150 --> 00:15:50,815
The last of the night drained
out of the sky.

203
00:15:51,385 --> 00:15:57,184
And on the third floor of a building nobody had ever
wondered about, Umbrella's new director

204
00:15:57,204 --> 00:15:58,006
went to work.
